# id: BM100-batch-keeps-what-the-range-covered
# blinds: a DeleteRange covering keys written EARLIER IN THE SAME BATCH
# covering-lane: cpp-test
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-build
# expect-control: alive
#
# THE HALF OF THE INTRA-BATCH RULE THE TOMBSTONE CANNOT EXPRESS. Every op in one
# batch shares a sequence, and the tombstone hides only sequences BELOW its own
# -- so a Set issued before the DeleteRange, at the same sequence, is not hidden
# by it and must be removed here or it survives.
#
# The result is a batch that is not atomic in the way the caller asked for: part
# of it was undone by a later op in the same batch and part was not.
--- a/engine-cpp/src/db.cc
+++ b/engine-cpp/src/db.cc
@@ -265,10 +265,7 @@
         // ceases to exist. B2 turned these into point deletions; there is no
         // need now, because the tombstone at this sequence hides whatever the
         // key held before the batch, and nothing in the batch survives it.
-        for (auto it = pending.begin(); it != pending.end();) {
-          it = InRange(Slice(it->first), start, e.end) ? pending.erase(it)
-                                                       : std::next(it);
-        }
+        // BM100: earlier ops in this batch are left alone.
         // AN UNBOUNDED START IS THE EMPTY KEY. The empty user key is the
         // minimum, so `["", end)` and `[unbounded, end)` cover the same set --
         // which is why only the END needed a representation (B3-Q4).
